$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/control-plane-api
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  The Control Plane API is the authority service: it allocates object ids, issues and revokes
  capability tokens, evaluates policy, and exposes the audit log. Sidecars and CLIs authenticate to
  it with an API key in the MVP and mTLS later. It never accepts or stores plaintext.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/control-plane
status: planned
intent: |
  Tokens need an issuing authority, policy needs a central place to live and change, and audit needs
  an append-only home. Centralising those makes the sidecar simple and stateless about authority,
  and keeps the only network-exposed component entirely outside the decryption path.

flows:
  - id: allocate-object
    name: Allocate an object id and wrapping policy
    description: The sidecar asks the control plane to register a new secret object.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar requests an object id with an intended wrapping policy and owning principal.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Authenticate the sidecar and authorise object creation for that principal.
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: Allocate and persist an object id and its policy record.
      - id: s4
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar receives the object id; no secret material is involved.
  - id: issue-token
    name: Issue a capability token
    description: The control plane mints a signed token for an object, scope, and audience.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Receive a mint request with object id, scope, audience, and TTL.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Evaluate policy for the requester, scope, object, and audience.
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: Build claims and sign the token with the active kid.
      - id: s4
        action: record
        target: Record the jti and the issuance event.
      - id: s5
        action: send
        target: Return the token URI.
  - id: revoke-token
    name: Revoke a token
    description: The control plane adds a jti to the denylist.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Receive a revoke request for a jti or object.
      - id: s2
        action: record
        target: Add to the denylist and write an audit record.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: Subsequent resolves with that jti are denied.

routes:
  - path: /v1/objects
    type: api
    protocol: rest
    http_methods: [POST]
    description: Allocate a new secret object and its wrapping policy.
  - path: /v1/tokens
    type: api
    protocol: rest
    http_methods: [POST]
    description: Issue a capability token for an object, scope, and audience.
  - path: /v1/tokens/{jti}
    type: api
    protocol: rest
    http_methods: [DELETE]
    description: Revoke a token by its unique id.
  - path: /v1/audit
    type: api
    protocol: rest
    http_methods: [GET]
    description: Query the append-only audit log.
  - path: /v1/policies
    type: api
    protocol: rest
    http_methods: [GET, PUT]
    description: Read and update resolution policy.
  - path: /healthz
    type: api
    protocol: rest
    http_methods: [GET]
    description: Liveness and readiness probe.

contracts:
  - id: no-plaintext-in-api
    description: No endpoint may accept or return plaintext secret material.
    must_have:
      - Request and response bodies carry identifiers, scopes, and metadata only
      - The API has no create-with-value endpoint
      - Contract enforced by schema tests on every handler
  - id: authenticated-clients
    description: Every endpoint requires an authenticated, authorised client.
    must_have:
      - API key authentication in the MVP; mTLS for sidecars later
      - Per-client scopes limit which operations are reachable
      - Unauthenticated requests are rejected before any state change
  - id: idempotent-issue-and-revoke
    description: Issue and revoke tolerate retries without duplicating or corrupting state.
    must_have:
      - Revoking an already-revoked jti is not an error
      - A retried issue does not mint two live tokens for one logical request
      - State changes are transactional in Postgres
  - id: token-issuance-is-audited
    description: Every issuance and revocation is recorded.
    must_have:
      - Principal, object, scope, audience, jti, and timestamp are recorded
      - Records are append-only
      - Records never contain secret material

tests:
  - id: no-endpoint-accepts-plaintext
    flow: allocate-object
    setup:
      attempt: "POST /v1/objects with a value field"
    expect:
      - assertion: the request is rejected
      - assertion: no value is persisted
    contracts:
      - no-plaintext-in-api
  - id: unauthenticated-request-rejected
    flow: issue-token
    setup:
      credentials: none
    expect:
      - assertion: the request is rejected with an authentication error
      - assertion: no token is minted
    contracts:
      - authenticated-clients
  - id: revoke-twice-is-safe
    flow: revoke-token
    setup:
      revoke_calls: 2
    expect:
      - assertion: both calls succeed
      - assertion: the jti is denied
    contracts:
      - idempotent-issue-and-revoke
  - id: issuance-audited
    flow: issue-token
    expect:
      - assertion: an audit record exists for the issuance
      - assertion: it contains the jti and scope
    contracts:
      - token-issuance-is-audited

decisions:
  - id: go-and-postgres
    decision: Implement the control plane as a Go service on Postgres.
    rationale: A single static binary and a battle-tested transactional store, sharing a toolchain with the sidecar and CLI. Policy and audit both need transactions, which rules out a simpler embedded store for the MVP.
    status: accepted
  - id: no-create-with-value-endpoint
    decision: Deliberately omit any endpoint that accepts a secret value.
    rationale: "The API's shape is part of the security design — if values cannot be posted, integrators cannot accidentally route plaintext through the control plane. Value creation belongs on the local sidecar channel."
    status: accepted
  - id: api-key-then-mtls
    decision: Start with API keys and add mTLS for sidecars alongside hardware binding.
    rationale: API keys are enough for an MVP where the sidecar is operator-installed, and mTLS earns its complexity only once device identity needs to be attested.
    status: accepted

implementation:
  primary: apps/control-plane/internal/api
  test_code: ""
  test_code_status: none

see_also:
  - "smith-gray/capability-token"
  - "smith-gray/policy-engine"
  - "smith-gray/audit-logging"
